This is the structure used by LeetCode and by everything in this material:
struct TreeNode {
int val;
TreeNode *left;
TreeNode *right;
TreeNode(int x) : val(x), left(NULL), right(NULL) {}
};
Three fields. A value, a pointer to the left child, a pointer to the right child. NULL means “there is no
child there”.
Two observations that carry the whole course:
1. A tree is just a pointer to its root. There is no separate Tree object. When a function takes
TreeNode* root , it has received an entire tree.
2. Every node is the root of its own subtree. So root->left is not “a child”, it is “a smaller tree”.
Passing root->left into the same function is completely legal and completely meaningful.
That is the whole idea behind tree recursion. Nothing more.
