Iterative preorder
This is the easy one, because preorder records a node the moment it arrives. There is nothing to
postpone about the node itself, only about its children.
Algorithm: push the root. Repeatedly pop a node, record it, then push its right child and then its left
child.
vector<int> preorderTraversal(TreeNode* root) {
vector<int> out;
if (root == NULL) return out;
stack<TreeNode*> st;
st.push(root);
while (!st.empty()) {
TreeNode* node = st.top();
st.pop();
out.push_back(node->val);
if (node->right) st.push(node->right); // right first
if (node->left) st.push(node->left); // left on top, so it pops first
}
return out;
}
Why right is pushed before left
A stack reverses order. We want left to be processed first, so left must be on top, so left goes in last.

vector<int> inorderTraversal(TreeNode* root) {
vector<int> out;
stack<TreeNode*> st;
TreeNode* curr = root;
while (curr != NULL || !st.empty()) {
while (curr != NULL) { // go left as far as possible
st.push(curr);
curr = curr->left;
}
curr = st.top(); // leftmost unfinished node
st.pop();
out.push_back(curr->val);
curr = curr->right; // switch to the right subtree
}
return out;
}
The loop condition
while (curr != NULL || !st.empty()) looks odd until you name the two situations:
• curr != NULL means “there is a subtree I have not started yet”
• !st.empty() means “there are nodes I postponed and still owe an answer to”

