Same complexity , one extra stack. Some people find it clearer. It is the same algorithm as method 1.
Method 3, one stack with a lastVisited pointer
This one produces true postorder in real time, but it needs one extra idea: remember which node
you most recently finished, so that when you come back up to a node you can tell whether its right
subtree is already done.
vector<int> postorderOneStack(TreeNode* root) {
vector<int> out;
stack<TreeNode*> st;
TreeNode* curr = root;
TreeNode* lastVisited = NULL;
while (curr != NULL || !st.empty()) {
while (curr != NULL) { // dive left, same as inorder
st.push(curr);
curr = curr->left;
}
TreeNode* node = st.top();
if (node->right != NULL && lastVisited != node->right) {
curr = node->right; // right subtree not done yet, go there
} else {
out.push_back(node->val); // both subtrees done, record now
lastVisited = node;
st.pop();
}

curr = node->right; // right subtree not done yet, go there
} else {
out.push_back(node->val); // both subtrees done, record now
lastVisited = node;
st.pop();
}
}
return out;
}
The condition lastVisited != node->right is the whole trick. It answers the question “have I just
come back from my own right child?” If yes, the node is finished. If no, the right subtree still has to
run.
Time O(N), space O(H).
Use method 1 in a contest. Use method 3 if the interviewer specifically asks for postorder without
reversing, or if you must process nodes in true postorder while traversing.
