Notice a recurring split in the last few rows. There are two different quantities:
• the answer through me, which may involve both of my children, and
• the value I can give my parent , which can only continue through one child, because a path
cannot go down the left, come back, and go down the right.
Diameter and maximum path sum are the classic examples, and confusing these two is the number
one error in this whole topic. We deal with it properly in Part 7.
Two ways to carry extra information
Way 1: return a small struct
Clean, explicit, and easy to explain in an interview.
struct Info {
int height;
int count;
};
Info solve(TreeNode* root) {
if (root == NULL) return {0, 0};
Info L = solve(root->left);
Info R = solve(root->right);
Info me;
me.height = 1 + max(L.height, R.height);
me.count = 1 + L.count + R.count;
return me;
}
