Collecting the actual paths: backtracking
LeetCode 257 asks for all root to leaf paths, printed. Now we need the path itself, not just a number.
Storing a vector<int> by value at every call would be O(N) copying per node. Instead keep one
shared vector and undo your change on the way out.
void dfs(TreeNode* root, vector<int>& path, vector<vector<int>>& res) {
if (root == NULL) return;
path.push_back(root->val); // do
if (root->left == NULL && root->right == NULL) {
res.push_back(path); // record a copy
} else {
dfs(root->left, path, res);
dfs(root->right, path, res);
}
path.pop_back(); // undo
}
The push_back and pop_back must pair up. That pairing is what backtracking means: the shared
state is exactly correct when you enter a node and exactly restored when you leave it.
